<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="subsection-even-stronger-forms-of-metric-continuity" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Even Stronger Forms of Metric Continuity
  </title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      We conclude this section with a look at a stronger form of continuity than uniform continuity. A map between metric spaces is called <term> Lipschitz continuous </term> if it changes the distance between points by at most some positive scalar multiple.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <definition xml:id="definition-lipschitz-continuity">
    <title>
      Lipschitz continuity
    </title>
    
    <idx>
      lipschitz continuity
    </idx>
    
    <notation>
      <usage>
        <m> \LC \of{ X , Y } </m>
      </usage>
      
      <description>
        <p>
          the set of Lipschitz continuous maps from <m> X </m> to <m> Y </m>
        </p>
      </description>
    </notation>
    
    <notation>
      <usage>
        <m> \LC \of{ X } </m>
      </usage>
      
      <description>
        <p>
          the set of Lipschitz continuous real-valued functions on <m> X </m>
        </p>
      </description>
    </notation>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        A map <m> f </m> from a metric space <m> \of{ X , \rho_{ X } } </m> to a metric space <m> \of{ Y , \rho_{ Y } } </m> is called <term> Lipschitz continuous </term> if there is a non-negative real number <m> L \geq 0 </m> so that
        
        <md>
          d_{ Y } \of{ f \of{ x_{ 1 } } , f \of{ x_{ 2 } } } \leq L d_{ X } \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } }
        </md>
        
        for all points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in X </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        The set of Lipschitz continuous maps from a metric space <m> \of{ X , \rho_{ X } } </m> to a metric space <m> \of{ Y , \rho_{ Y } } </m> is denoted <m> \LC \of{ X , Y } </m>, and the set of Lipschitz continuous real-valued functions on <m> \of{ X , \rho_{ X } } </m> is denoted <m> \LC \of{ X } = \LC \of{ X , \R } </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  
  <p>
    As with uniform continuity, Lipschitz continuity does not apply at a specific point, but rather over the entire domain of a map. <alert> It does not make sense to say that a map between metric spaces is Lipschitz continuous at a point. </alert>
  </p>

  <example xml:id="example-norms-are-Lipschitz-continuous">
    <title>
      Norms are Lipschitz continuous
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> \of{ V , \norm{ \oparg } } </m> be a normed vector space. The <xref ref="proposition-reverse-triangle-inequality" text="title"/> implies that 
        
        <md>
          d \of{ \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 1 } } , \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 2 } } } = \abs{ \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 1 } } - \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 2 } } } \leq \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 1 } - \vect{ v }_{ 2 } } = 1 \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 1 } - \vect{ v }_{ 2 } }
        </md>
        
        for all vectors <m> \vect{ v }_{ 1 } , \vect{ v }_{ 2 } \in V </m>, and so the norm <m> \norm{ \oparg } \colon V \to \R </m> is Lipschitz continuous.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>
  
  <p>
    For any Lipschitz continuous map between metric spaces, there is a unique smallest non-negative real number such that the above condition is satisfied. This unique smallest constant is called the <term> Lipschitz constant </term> for the map.
  </p>
  
  <definition xml:id="definition-lipschitz-constant">
    <title>
      Lipschitz constant
    </title>
    
    <idx>
      Lipschitz constant
    </idx>
    
    <notation>
      <usage>
        <m> L_{ f } </m>
      </usage>
      
      <description>
        <p>
          the Lipschitz constant of <m> f </m>
        </p>
      </description>
    </notation>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        The <term> Lipschitz constant </term> <m> L_{ f } </m> of a map <m> f \colon X \to Y </m> from a metric space <m> \of{ X , \rho_{ X } } </m> to a metric space <m> \of{ Y , \rho_{ Y } } </m> is the infimum
        
        <md>
          L_{ f } = \inf \of{ \setmid{ L \in \clopint{ 0 }{ \infty } }{ \rho_{ Y } \of{ f \of{ x_{ 1 } } , f \of{ x_{ 2 } } } \leq L \rho_{ X } \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } \textrm{ for all } x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in X } }
        </md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  
  <proposition xml:id="proposition-lipschitz-constants-and-lipschitz-continuity">
    <title>
      Lipschitz constants and Lipschitz continuity
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        A map <m> f \colon X \to Y </m> from a metric space <m> \of{ X , \rho_{ X } } </m> to a metric space <m> \of{ Y , \rho_{ Y } } </m> is Lipschitz continuous if and only if <m> L_{ f } \lt \infty </m>, in which case
        
        <md>
          \rho_{ Y } \of{ f \of{ x_{ 1 } } , f \of{ x_{ 2 } } } \leq L_{ f } \rho_{ X } \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } }
        </md>
        
        for all points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in X </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <p>
        We observe that
        
        <md>
          C = \setmid{ L \in \clopint{ 0 }{ \infty } }{ \rho_{ Y } \of{ f \of{ x_{ 1 } } , f \of{ x_{ 2 } } } \leq L \rho_{ X } \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } \textrm{ for all } x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in X }
        </md>
        
        is a subset of the real numbers, and so <m> L_{ f } = \inf \of{ C } \lt \infty </m> if and only if <m> C \neq \emptyset </m> is non-empty. That <m> C \neq \emptyset </m> is non-empty is precisely the Lipschitz continuity condition for <m> f </m>, and so <m> f </m> is Lipschitz continuous if and only if <m> L_{ f } \lt \infty </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        If <m> C \neq \emptyset </m> is non-empty, let <m> \of{ l_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> be a sequence of elements <m> l_{ n } \in L </m> which converge to <m> L_{ f } = \inf \of{ C } </m>. We observe that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \rho_{ Y } \of{ f \of{ x_{ 1 } } , f \of{ x_{ 2 } } } \amp = \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ \rho_{ Y } \of{ f \of{ x_{ 1 } } , f \of{ x_{ 2 } } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp \leq \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ l_{ n } \rho_{ X } \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } } = L_{ f } \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } }
          </mrow>
        </md>
        
        for all points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in X </m>.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  
  <p>
    A map is Lipschitz continuous if it increases the distance between points by at most some nonnegative constant multiplier. This implies that Lipschitz continuity is a stronger condition than uniform continuity, and therefore is also a stronger condition than continuity.
  </p>
  
  <proposition xml:id="proposition-lipschitz-continuity-implies-uniform-continuity">
    <title>
      Lipschitz continuity implies uniform continuity
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        If a map between metric spaces is Lipschitz continuous, then it is uniformly continuous and continuous.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <p>
        Let <m> f \colon X \to Y </m> be a map from a metric space <m> \of{ X , \rho_{ X } } </m> to a metric space <m> \of{ Y , \rho_{ Y } } </m>, and consider a positive real number <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m>. There are two cases; either <m> L_{ f } = 0 </m>, or <m> 0 \lt L_{ f } \lt \infty </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        If <m> L_{ f } = 0 </m>, then <xref ref="proposition-lipschitz-constants-and-lipschitz-continuity"/> implies that
        
        <md>
          0 \leq \rho_{ Y } \of{ f \of{ x_{ 1 } } , f \of{ x_{ 2 } } } \leq L_{ f } \rho_{ X } \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } = 0 \rho_{ X } \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } = 0 \lt \epsilon
        </md>
        
        for all points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in X </m>, and so <m> f \of{ x_{ 1 } } = f \of{ x_{ 2 } } </m> for all points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in X </m>. In particular, this implies that <m> f </m> is constant and therefore uniformly continuous.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        If on the other hand <m> 0 \lt L_{ f } \lt \infty </m>, let <m> \delta = \frac{ \epsilon }{ L_{ f } } </m>, and note that <xref ref="proposition-lipschitz-constants-and-lipschitz-continuity"/> implies that
        
        <md>
          0 \leq \rho_{ Y } \of{ f \of{ x_{ 1 } } , f \of{ x_{ 2 } } } \leq L_{ f } \rho_{ X } \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } \lt L_{ f } \of{ \frac{ \epsilon }{ L_{ f } } } = \epsilon
        </md>
        
        for all points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in X </m> so that <m> \rho_{ X } \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } \lt \delta </m>. Thus <m> f </m> is uniformly continuous. We conclude that <m> f </m> is continuous by <xref ref="proposition-uniform-continuity-implies-continuity"/>.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  
  <example xml:id="example-uniform-continuity-does-not-imply-lipschitz-continuity">
    <title>
      Uniform continuity does not imply Lipschitz continuity
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the real-valued function <m> f \colon \clopint{ 0 }{ \infty } \to \R </m> defined by the formula <m> f \of{ x } = \sqrt{ x } </m>. For each positive real number <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m>, let <m> \delta = \epsilon^{ 2 } \gt 0 </m>. We observe that
        
        <md>
          \abs{ \sqrt{ x_{ 1 } } + \sqrt{ x_{ 2 } } } \leq \abs{ \sqrt{ x_{ 1 } } } + \abs{ \sqrt{ x_{ 2 } } } = \sqrt{ x_{ 1 } } + \sqrt{ x_{ 2 } } = \abs{ \sqrt{ x_{ 1 } } + \sqrt{ x_{ 2 } } }
        </md>
        
        for all points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in \clopint{ 0 }{ \infty } </m>, and so
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            d \of{ f \of{ x_{ 1 } } , f \of{ x_{ 2 } } }^{ 2 } \amp = \abs{ \sqrt{ x_{ 1 } } - \sqrt{ x_{ 2 } } }^{ 2 } \leq \abs{ \sqrt{ x_{ 1 } } + \sqrt{ x_{ 2 } } } \cdot \abs{ \sqrt{ x_{ 1 } } - \sqrt{ x_{ 2 } } } = \abs{ x_{ 1 } - x_{ 2 } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = d \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } \lt \delta = \epsilon^{ 2 }
          </mrow>
        </md>
        
        for all points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in \clopint{ 0 }{ \infty } </m> so that <m> d \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } \lt \delta </m>. In particular, <m> d \of{ f \of{ x_{ 1 } } , f \of{ x_{ 2 } } } \lt \epsilon </m> for all points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in \clopint{ 0 }{ \infty } </m> so that <m> d \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } \lt \delta </m>, and so <m> f </m> is uniformly continuous.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        On the other hand, we observe that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \frac{ d \of{ f \of{ \frac{ 1 }{ \of{ L + 1 }^{ 2 } } } , f \of{ 0 } } }{ d \of{ \frac{ 1 }{ \of{ L + 1 }^{ 2 } } , 0 } } \amp = \frac{ \abs{ \sqrt{ \frac{ 1 }{ \of{ L + 1 }^{ 2 } } } - \sqrt{ 0 } } }{ \abs{ \frac{ 1 }{ \of{ L + 1 }^{ 2 } } - 0 } } = \abs{ \frac{ \of{ \frac{ 1 }{ L + 1 } } }{ \of{ \frac{ 1 }{ \of{ L + 1 }^{ 2 } } } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \abs{ \frac{ \of{ L + 1 }^{ 2 } }{ L + 1 } } = \abs{ L + 1 } = L + 1 \gt L
          </mrow>
        </md>
        
        for any non-negative real number <m> 0 \leq L \lt \infty </m>, so that
        
        <md>
          d \of{ f \of{ \frac{ 1 }{ \of{ L + 1 }^{ 2 } } } , f \of{ 0 } } \gt L d \of{ \frac{ 1 }{ \of{ L + 1 }^{ 2 } } , 0 }
        </md>.
        
        Thus <m> L_{ f } \gt L </m>. Since this holds for each non-negative real number <m> 0 \leq L \lt \infty </m>, <m> L_{ f } = \infty </m> and so <m> f </m> is not Lipschitz continuous by <xref ref="proposition-lipschitz-constants-and-lipschitz-continuity"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>
  
  <p>
    Just as in the case of continuity and uniform continuity, Lipschitz continuity is preserved by composition, although possibly at the cost of increasing the Lipschitz constant.
  </p>
  
  <lemma xml:id="lemma-lipschitz-continuity-and-composition">
    <title>
      Lipschitz continuity and composition
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> f \colon X \to Y </m> and <m> g \colon Y \to Z </m> be maps between metric spaces <m> \of{ X , \rho_{ X } } </m>, <m> \of{ Y , \rho_{ Y } } </m>, and <m> \of{ Z , \rho_{ Z } } </m>. If <m> f </m> and <m> g </m> are Lipschitz continuous, then so is their composition <m> g \circ f \colon X \to Z </m>, and <m> L_{ g \circ f } \leq L_{ g } L_{ f } </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <p>
        We observe that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \rho_{ Z } \of{ \of{ g \circ f } \of{ x_{ 1 } } , \of{ g \circ f } \of{ x_{ 2 } } } \amp = \rho_{ Z } \of{ g \of{ f \of{ x_{ 1 } } } , g \of{ f \of{ x_{ 2 } } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp \leq L_{ g } \rho_{ Y } \of{ f \of{ x_{ 1 } } , f \of{ x_{ 2 } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp \leq L_{ g } L_{ f } \rho_{ X } \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } }
          </mrow>
        </md>
        
        for all points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in X </m>. We conclude that the composition <m> g \circ f </m> is Lipschitz continuous and <m> L_{ g \circ f } \leq L_{ g } L_{ f } </m>.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </lemma>
  
  <p>
    Just as in the case of continuity and uniform continuity, Lipschitz continuous maps from a metric space to a normed vector space form a vector space under the pointwise operations of addition and scalar multiplication, although possibly at the cost of increasing the Lipschitz constant.
  </p>
  
  <proposition xml:id="proposition-vector-arithmetic-and-lipschitz-continuity">
    <title>
      Vector arithmetic and Lipschitz continuity
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> \of{ X , \rho } </m> be a metric space and <m> \of{ V , \norm{ \oparg } } </m> be a normed vector space.
        
        <ol>
          <li>
            <title>
              Vector addition
            </title>
            
            <p>
              If two maps <m> f , g \colon X \to V </m> from <m> X </m> to <m> Y </m> are Lipschitz continuous, then their pointwise sum <m> f + g \colon X \to V </m> is also Lipschitz continuous, and <m> L_{ f + g } \leq L_{ f } + L_{ g } </m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <title>
              Scalar multiplication
            </title>
            
            <p>
              If a map <m> f \colon X \to V </m> is Lipschitz continuous, then any pointwise scalar multiple <m> \alpha f </m> is also Lipschitz continuous, and <m> L_{ \alpha f } \leq \abs{ \alpha } L_{ f } </m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <title>
              Norm
            </title>
            
            <p>
              If a map <m> f \colon X \to V </m> is uniformly continuous, then its pointwise norm <m> \norm{ f } = \norm{ \oparg } \circ f </m> is also uniformly continuous, and <m> L_{ \norm{ f } } \leq L_{ f } </m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            We observe that
            
            <md>
              <mrow>
                \norm{ \of{ f + g } \of{ x_{ 1 } } - \of{ f + g } \of{ x_{ 2 } } } \amp = \norm{ f \of{ x_{ 1 } } + g \of{ x_{ 1 } } - f \of{ x_{ 2 } } - g \of{ x_{ 2 } } }
              </mrow>
              
              <mrow>
                \amp \leq \norm{ f \of{ x_{ 1 } } - f \of{ x_{ 2 } } } + \norm{ g \of{ x_{ 1 } } - g \of{ x_{ 2 } } }
              </mrow>
              
              <mrow>
                \amp \leq L_{ f } \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } + L_{ g } \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } }
              </mrow>
              
              <mrow>
                \amp = \of{ L_{ f } + L_{ g } } \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } }
              </mrow>
            </md>
            
            for all points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in X </m>. We conclude that the composition <m> g \circ f </m> is Lipschitz continuous and <m> L_{ f + g } \leq L_{ f } + L_{ g } </m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        
        <li>
          <p>
            We observe that
            
            <md>
              <mrow>
                \norm{ \of{ \alpha f } \of{ x_{ 1 } } - \of{ \alpha f } \of{ x_{ 2 } } } \amp = \norm{ \alpha f \of{ x_{ 1 } } - \alpha f \of{ x_{ 2 } } } = \norm{ \alpha \of{ f \of{ x_{ 1 } } - f \of{ x_{ 2 } } } }
              </mrow>
              
              <mrow>
                \amp \leq \abs{ \alpha } \norm{ f \of{ x_{ 1 } } - f \of{ x_{ 2 } } } \leq \abs{ \alpha } L_{ f } \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } }
              </mrow>
            </md>
            
            for all points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in X </m>. We conclude that the pointwise scalar multiple <m> \alpha f </m> is Lipschitz continuous and <m> L_{ \alpha f } \leq \abs{ \alpha } L_{ f } </m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        
        <li>
          <p>
            Recall from <xref ref="example-norms-are-Lipschitz-continuous"/> that the norm <m> \norm{ \oparg } \colon V \to \R </m> is Lipschitz continuous with Lipschitz constant <m> L_{ \norm{ \oparg } } \leq 1 </m>. Thus <xref ref="lemma-lipschitz-continuity-and-composition"/> implies that the composition <m> \norm{ f } = \norm{ \oparg } \circ f </m> is Lipschitz continuous, with Lipschitz constant
            
            <md>
              L_{ \norm{ f } } = L_{ \norm{ \oparg } \circ f } \leq L_{ \norm{ \oparg } } L_{ f } \leq 1 L_{ f } = L_{ f }
            </md>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </proof>
  </proposition>

  <conclusion>
    <p>
      You will have several opportunities to explore applications of Lipschitz continuity in the problems for both this section and <xref ref="section-completeness-and-compactness"/>.
    </p>
  </conclusion>
</subsection>
